            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_local_content",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgGeo21\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgGeo21', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Africa is highlighted on this map © Mapswire (<htcite0>)</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Introduction</h3>Before we pin down our location on Earth, consider a question:</p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: are we using the right number of variables?</b><br /><br />Could we be oversimplifying by using only longitude (<b>θ</b>) and latitude (<b>δ</b>) to locate ourselves on the Earth’s surface? Keep your guess: we will check it at the end.</div></div></center><h3>Our Coordinates</h3><p>Now we will turn angles into meters, so that our position can also be expressed as distances.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>Simplification</h4>When we use only latitude and longitude to locate ourselves, we really are simplifying. The simplification assumes that the Earth’s average radius is constant: about 6,371 kilometers (km), or 6,371,000 meters (m) — the average distance from the Earth’s center to its surface",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fd003897-89a6-4d01-ae79-da9e564d9583",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "924f3991-5eec-4639-b307-5ce39447f6f6",
                     "text": "NASA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "To better understand this simplification, let’s look at an example. By assuming a constant Earth radius, we do not remove it from our calculations; we only leave it out of the final coordinates. That is why we can express the locations of cities like Tokyo (<htcite0>) and Montevideo (<htcite1>) with latitude and longitude alone",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1804b247-7fe8-4cff-a4e2-91accbaa936a",
                     "text": "Google Search",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fb327056-722a-41e9-9f72-e1e77a5318c9",
                     "text": "Google Search",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"><li><b>Tokyo</b>: (35.6764° North, 139.6500° East)</li><li><b>Montevideo</b>: (34.9055° South, 56.1851° West)</li></ul></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>Variable transformation</h4><p>Until now we have described the position of a point with angles and a radius. Here we swap those variables for distances along the axes — the Cartesian coordinates. We do it in two steps, always starting from a right triangle: first for a point on a flat plane, and then for a point on a sphere.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 700 620\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:760px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"A circle of radius r with a point at the angle theta. The horizontal leg of the right triangle, x, is r times cosine of theta, and the vertical leg, y, is r times sine of theta\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">x = r cos(θ)</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">y = r sin(θ)</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"660\" y2=\"420\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"660,427 676,420 660,413\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"600\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"247,60 240,44 233,60\" fill=\"#555555\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"420\" r=\"180\" fill=\"#eaf2fa\" fill-opacity=\"0.45\" stroke=\"#4f8bc4\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><polygon points=\"240,420 377.9,420 377.9,304.3\" fill=\"#fff4e0\" fill-opacity=\"0.85\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"377.9\" y2=\"420\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"377.9\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"377.9\" y2=\"304.3\" stroke=\"#27ae60\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"377.9\" y2=\"304.3\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 310,420 Q 313.3,393.3 293.6,375\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"322\" y=\"398\" font-size=\"22\" font-style=\"italic\" fill=\"#333333\">θ</text><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"420\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><circle cx=\"377.9\" cy=\"304.3\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><text x=\"222\" y=\"442\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#333333\">O</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#333333\">P</text><text x=\"668\" y=\"448\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333333\">X</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333333\">Y</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"346\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#c0392b\">r</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"452\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\">x = r cos(θ)</text><text x=\"430\" y=\"380\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#27ae60\">y = r sin(θ)</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: A point on a plane written with the radius <b>r</b> and the angle <b>θ</b>: the adjacent leg is <b>x</b> = r × cos(θ) and the opposite leg is <b>y</b> = r × sin(θ).</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>On a plane, a point <b>P</b> is described by the radius <b>r</b> and by the angle <b>θ</b>. Weisstein, Eric W. presents this transformation (<htcite0>) as a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the radius. Let us follow the steps:</p><ol><li>The radius <b>r</b> goes from the origin <b>O</b> to the point <b>P</b>, and the angle <b>θ</b> is measured between the x-axis and the radius.</li><li>We project the point on the x-axis: the adjacent leg of the triangle measures <b>r</b> × cos(<b>θ</b>); therefore, <b>x</b> = r × cos(θ).</li><li>We project the point on the y-axis: the opposite leg measures <b>r</b> × sin(<b>θ</b>); therefore, <b>y</b> = r × sin(θ).</li></ol><p>Thus the same point can be written as (r, θ) or as (x, y), and the radius is recovered by r<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d94b147e-c84b-40cc-8a86-5d18cefc9c59",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Polar Coordinates",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>The transformation above solves the problem on a flat surface. However, a point in space is not always on that surface: it can be above or below the plane. In the next step we add the third coordinate and repeat the same idea on a sphere, where the radius <b>r</b> and a new angle <b>ϕ</b>, measured from the vertical axis, tell us how high or low the point is.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 620\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:760px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"A transparent sphere with a point P on its surface and its projection Q on the flat plane. The angle phi is measured from the vertical axis and the angle theta on the plane, giving the coordinates x, y and z of the point\"><text x=\"20\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">x = r sin(ϕ) cos(θ)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">y = r sin(ϕ) sin(θ)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#333333\">z = r cos(ϕ)</text><defs><radialGradient id=\"ht53089395SphereGrad\" cx=\"0.32\" cy=\"0.22\" r=\"1.05\"><stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#ffffff\" stop-opacity=\"0.55\"/><stop offset=\"45%\" stop-color=\"#a9c9e8\" stop-opacity=\"0.28\"/><stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#2f6fb3\" stop-opacity=\"0.10\"/></radialGradient></defs><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"390\" r=\"185\" fill=\"url(#ht53089395SphereGrad)\" stroke=\"#4f8bc4\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"390\" rx=\"185\" ry=\"55\" fill=\"#eaf2fa\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#4f8bc4\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"390\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"185\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4f8bc4\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.35\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"390\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"142.7\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"330,142.7 337,158.7 323,158.7\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"390\" x2=\"632.5\" y2=\"443.2\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"632.5,443.2 615.5,447.3 617.9,433.5\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"390\" x2=\"508.9\" y2=\"300.1\" stroke=\"#555555\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"508.9,300.1 497.7,313.6 491.5,301.0\" fill=\"#555555\"/><line x1=\"428.1\" y1=\"234.7\" x2=\"428.1\" y2=\"370.0\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"390\" x2=\"428.1\" y2=\"370.0\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/><line x1=\"428.1\" y1=\"370.0\" x2=\"373.3\" y2=\"397.6\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/><line x1=\"428.1\" y1=\"370.0\" x2=\"384.9\" y2=\"362.4\" stroke=\"#2f6fb3\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"390\" x2=\"428.1\" y2=\"234.7\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 330,310 Q 355,303.6 372.7,322.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"373\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"22\" font-style=\"italic\" fill=\"#333333\">ϕ</text><path d=\"M 384.2,399.5 Q 392.0,389.2 383.9,379.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"404\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"22\" font-style=\"italic\" fill=\"#333333\">θ</text><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"390\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><circle cx=\"428.1\" cy=\"234.7\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><circle cx=\"428.1\" cy=\"370.0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#333333\"/><text x=\"312\" y=\"412\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#333333\">O</text><text x=\"441\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#333333\">P</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"362\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#333333\">Q</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333333\">Z</text><text x=\"648\" y=\"462\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333333\">X</text><text x=\"523\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#333333\">Y</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"340\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#c0392b\">r</text></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: A transparent sphere with the projection of the point <b>P</b> on the flat plane (point <b>Q</b>). The angle <b>ϕ</b> is measured from the vertical axis and the angle <b>θ</b> from the x axis, giving x = r × sin(ϕ) × cos(θ), y = r × sin(ϕ) × sin(θ) and z = r × cos(ϕ).</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>For a point in space we need a third coordinate. The radius <b>r</b> is the distance from the origin, <b>θ</b> is the angle on the flat plane and <b>ϕ</b> is the angle measured from the vertical axis. Weisstein, Eric W. describes this transformation step by step (<htcite0>):</p><ol><li>We project the point <b>P</b> from the sphere down to the plane and obtain the point <b>Q</b>. The projected radius <b>OQ</b> measures <b>r</b> × sin(<b>ϕ</b>), and the height of the point is <b>z</b> = r × cos(ϕ).</li><li>On the plane we apply the previous transformation to the projected radius: <b>x</b> = OQ × cos(<b>θ</b>) = r × sin(ϕ) × cos(θ).</li><li>In the same way, on the other axis: <b>y</b> = OQ × sin(<b>θ</b>) = r × sin(ϕ) × sin(θ).</li><li>Joining the three results gives (x, y, z) = (r × sin(ϕ) × cos(θ), r × sin(ϕ) × sin(θ), r × cos(ϕ)).</li></ol><p>Both transformations use the same idea twice: first the radius is projected on the plane, and then the projection is distributed across the two axes.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fd003897-89a6-4d01-ae79-da9e564d9583",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Spherical Coordinates",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>All variables</h4>To express coordinates more precisely, it’s essential to use the same unit of measurement for all coordinates. On the Earth’s surface, when we work with angles, we can simplify calculations by omitting the Earth’s radius. However, for an accurate three-dimensional position, we need to include the radius, transform the latitude (<b>δ</b>) into colatitude (<b>ϕ</b>) using ϕ = 90° − δ (<htcite0>), and convert the angles from degrees to radians (<htcite1>) before continuing with the calculations",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1cfb7c60-fa0b-4201-b34d-c9f0d3cca2d7",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Surface Coordinates",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fd003897-89a6-4d01-ae79-da9e564d9583",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>rad</mtext></msub><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>θ</mi><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>180</mi></mfrac></math><br /><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>rad</mtext></msub><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>90</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>180</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Next, we apply these converted angles in the formulas for spherical-to-Cartesian conversion, considering the radius <b>r</b>:</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center>(x, y, z) = (r cos(θ) sin(ϕ), r sin(θ) sin(ϕ), r cos(ϕ))",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fd003897-89a6-4d01-ae79-da9e564d9583",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ".</center>"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"><li><b>Tokyo</b>: (-3944126.27, 3350784.05, 3715609.67)</li><li><b>Montevideo</b>: (2907683.28, 4341002.95, -3645642.93)</li></ul></p><p>With this three-dimensional representation, we get a complete and much more precise location.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<h3>Conclusion</h3><p>In this text we made a big choice: we treated the Earth as a perfect sphere, like a ball, even though it is slightly flattened at the poles — an ellipsoid. This choice is a <b>simplification</b>, and it brings a very useful consequence. If the radius is always the same, we no longer need to worry about it. That is why any place on the surface can be described with only two angles: the <b>longitude</b> (θ) and the <b>latitude</b> (δ).</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>After that, we learned what to do when we need the position in meters instead of degrees. We made two more transformations. First, we changed the latitude into <b>colatitude</b> (ϕ = 90° − δ), because the spherical formulas measure the angle from the vertical axis. Then we used the transformation from <b>spherical coordinates</b> to <b>Cartesian coordinates</b> (x, y, z), applying the same right triangle twice.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Are these results wrong? No. They are very good approximations, and they help us do the math without getting lost in the details. Even so, they are approximations: to find the real position on the Earth's surface, small adjustments are made, because our planet is not a perfect sphere. The main idea is simple: each simplification brings us to a problem we can solve, and each adjustment brings us closer to the real world.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
